---
import KickoffSession from "../../components/KickoffSession.astro";

const photos = [
    {
        src: "/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5410.jpg",
        alt: "Yulia Korobkova speaking beside the podium. Her name is on the slide.",
        caption: "On data centres as a high-tech industrial campus.",
    },
];
---

<KickoffSession
    documentTitle="Canada has everything. | Canada2080"
    description="Yulia Korobkova on building data-centre infrastructure in Canada, from her cleaned remarks at the Canada2080 kickoff."
    eyebrow="Capacity · 1:55 p.m."
    headline="Canada has everything."
    name="Yulia Korobkova"
    bio="Yulia Korobkova is Data Centers Studio Leader and Vice President at Corgan in Toronto. Her practice combines architecture, project leadership, and the delivery of compute infrastructure."
    profileUrl="https://www.linkedin.com/in/yulia-korobkova/"
    podiumSrc="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5410.jpg"
    podiumAlt="Yulia Korobkova speaking beside the podium. Her name is on the slide."
    photos={photos}
>
    <p class="lede">
        She does not think the build waits until 2080. With the technology she
        sees, it is 2040 or 2050.
    </p>
    <p>
        She was travelling and had not planned a formal presentation. She
        thanked Francis for the invitation and spoke from more than 15 years of
        building industrial projects.
    </p>
    <p>
        Canada, she said, already has the land, the talent, the technology, and
        the manufacturing resources to lead in building infrastructure. That
        runs from the computing available for AI infrastructure to the
        construction available to build it.
    </p>
    <p>
        A data centre, in her comparison, has less environmental effect than
        light industry. She named a bottling plant and an Amazon distribution
        centre. Noise is lower than the distribution centre. With closed-loop
        systems, water use is about the same as an office. Contamination is
        almost nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
        She also sees a shift in real estate and development, from Class A
        buildings alone toward cohesive, high-end campuses the community is
        part of. Jobs are created. Buffer zones are provided. The building is
        done with the community, and with less impact on the environment.
    </p>
    <p>
        For her, a data centre is no different from any other industrial
        facility. It has to be done right. Canada has what that takes.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="transcript">
        <p class="transcript-note">Cleaned up transcripts.</p>
        <div class="transcript-columns">
            <div>
                <h3>Keynote</h3>
        <blockquote>
            <p>Hi everyone, can you hear me well?</p>
            <p>
                Yeah, I wasn't ready to present anything, to be honest. I'm
                travelling very much this week and next week. I will share my
                experience. Thank you, Francis, for inviting me to this great
                event. I think we have a strong voice. You put it in the
                perspective of 2080. I think with all the technology that is
                unfolding, it is actually a very dynamic unfolding perspective.
                I think it won't be 2080. I think it will be 2040 or 2050 with
                everything that I see on the market. Starting from the
                technology that is actually available for computing for the AI
                infrastructure, to what's available for construction purposes
                to build this infrastructure.
            </p>
            <p>
                To be very, very honest, I truly believe that in Canada, we
                have everything. We have land, we have talent, we have smart
                people and technology, and we have manufacturing resources. We
                have land, we have everything to proceed and to be leaders in
                building infrastructure.
            </p>
            <p>
                I know that there's a lot of speculative discussion about AI
                infrastructure and data centres. But I'm very thankful that I
                have more than 15 years of experience building industrial
                projects and industrial infrastructure. I truly believe that AI
                or data centres, a lot of people are talking about the
                environment, you know, how it affects the environment. If you
                compare a data centre with any other manufacturing facility,
                not even a heavy-duty manufacturing facility, but something like
                a bottling factory, like Coca-Cola, or an Amazon distribution
                centre, you will be very surprised that, compared to these
                light industrial facilities, data centres actually provide less
                environmental effect.
            </p>
            <p>
                Yes, there is noise, but it's less than any Amazon distribution
                centre. In terms of water, with the closed-loop systems and the
                technology available now, the impact will be the same as any
                office's water usage. As for contamination, it's almost
                nothing.
            </p>
            <p>
                I also see that there is a big shift from the real estate and
                developers' perspective, moving from not only Class A buildings
                but to cohesive, high-end, and community-involved campuses.
                What I mean by that is developing big industrial and high-tech
                campuses where the community is involved, jobs are created,
                proper buffer zones are provided, and building is done right
                with community involvement and less impact on the environment.
            </p>
            <p>
                For me, data centres are no different than any other industrial
                facility. We just need to make it right. I truly believe that
                Canada has everything to develop this infrastructure. Thank
                you.
            </p>
        </blockquote>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3 id="panel">Panel</h3>
                <p class="transcript-note">Francis Wang asked the questions.</p>
                <p class="ask">
                    How do we know we are making progress as a country?
                </p>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        Can I add to this point? I also think that from the
                        design perspective and architecture perspective: labour
                        harmonization, requirements harmonization, and
                        construction development charges dropping a little bit
                        will help developers do it faster, cohesive, and
                        easier. I think that's fine for parts of the country.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        Sounds strong. Disruption always comes from the
                        outside. I think it's good news.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>
                <p class="ask">What about Monday?</p>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        I think for a lot of businesses, it's learning to be
                        comfortable with being uncomfortable. Through all the
                        new actual measuring points, resilience is a new
                        measuring point, to be resilient as a business or
                        practice. Every day, look for new ways of doing stuff.
                        Where the constraints are, that opens up opportunity to
                        be more resilient and efficient, and to align with the
                        idea of moving forward outside your comfort zone.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>
            </div>
        </div>
    </Fragment>
</KickoffSession>
